From 403bffdde28ec82cd51ab077361e978a2d8cefa4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 02:24:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] swscale/ppc: complete the packed 4:2:2 unscaled converters

Restructure yuv2yuv_altivec.c into one av_always_inline template,
planar_to_packed422_altivec(), plus four thin wrappers:
yv12toyuy2_unscaled_altivec / yv12touyvy_unscaled_altivec (existing
names, vertLumPerChroma=2) and new yuv422ptoyuy2_unscaled_altivec /
yuv422ptouyvy_unscaled_altivec (vertLumPerChroma=1). vertLumPerChroma
and the uyvy byte-order flag are compile-time constants at every call
site, so av_always_inline folds away the dead half of every ternary
and produces the same specialized code the old hand-duplicated
functions had.

Each row now has three tiers instead of the old 32-wide-main +
16-wide-remainder split, so it writes exactly 4*(width>>1) bytes for
ANY width, not just multiples of 16, with no load touching an aligned
word past the last needed byte:
  1) 32 pixels/iteration while i+32<=width (as fixed in the previous
     commit, generalized to either byte order via the uyvy flag);
  2) 8 pixels/iteration while i+8<=(width&~1): luma via
     vec_perm(vec_ld(i),vec_ld(i+7),lvsl(i)) (8 bytes needed), chroma
     via vec_perm(vec_ld(j),vec_ld(j+3),lvsl(j)) (4 bytes needed each
     of u/v), one mergeh producing one output vector, one store;
  3) a scalar tail for the remaining 0..3 pixel pairs, written
     byte-by-byte in the target order -- exactly the C reference's
     per-pair stores, nothing wider that could overrun the row.
The per-row aligned/store16_unaligned choice from the previous commit
carries over unchanged (every store offset in a row is a multiple of
16, so one dst-alignment check per row covers every store in it).

Registration in ff_get_unscaled_swscale_ppc(): dropped the
`!(src_w & 15)` gate entirely (the kernel now handles any width);
kept `!(flags & SWS_BITEXACT)` and the AltiVec cpu-flag check; added
YUV422P as a source (selecting the new yuv422p* wrappers) alongside
YUV420P/YUVA420P (selecting the yv12* wrappers, as before). Comment
added noting this override is unconditional on scaler flags (upstream
behaviour unchanged since 2004) unlike the C table's LQ wrappers,
which only apply under SWS_FAST_BILINEAR|SWS_POINT.

Validated: qemu-ppc-static -cpu 7400, incremental -j1 build, warning-
free compile. --test=sw_yuv2yuv, seeds 1/42/777: "all 58 tests
passed" for every run (was "10 of 10 tests failed" pre-fix, "all 10
tests passed" after the previous commit's bugfix-only gate). --bench
lists all 66 dispatching combinations (3 sources x 2 dst byte orders
x 11 widths) with the _altivec suffix, e.g.
yuv420p_yuyv422_1920_altivec, yuv422p_uyvy422_8_altivec,
yuva420p_yuyv422_2_altivec. Full suite once, seed 42: 897 (prior
baseline) + 58 (new named checks from this test) = 955 expected,
"all 955 tests passed" -- confirmed exact.

Noted, not fixed (pre-existing upstream behaviour, unrelated to this
kernel): sws_getContext (utils.c:1221) silently drops SWS_FAST_BILINEAR
whenever srcW<8||dstW<=8, which defeats the C LQ-wrapper's flag gate
for yuv420p/yuva420p at width 2 and 8 specifically, leaving
convert_unscaled NULL on the C-flags checkasm pass. checkasm skips a
NULL reference, so our unconditional PPC override becomes its own
first-registered (vacuous) reference for exactly those 8 combinations
(yuv420p/yuva420p x {yuyv422,uyvy422} x {2,8}) -- 66 total dispatches
in the --bench listing, 58 real comparisons. yuv422p is unaffected
(its C wrappers are flag-independent) and genuinely checked at every
width including 2 and 8. The affected code path is the scalar tail
only (one byte-copy pixel pair), identical in shape to the yuv422p
path verified at the same widths, so it is correct by construction
even though checkasm cannot independently prove it there.

